It's clearly a feminine name, in fact the name "Rachel" #link("https://www.kveller.com/why-rachel-is-such-a-popular-name/")[is a popular name in Jewish culture].
@kveller-rachel

"Levin" on the other hand is a variation of a Eastern-European (Ashkenazi) Jewish surname,
where the root name "Levi"
#link("https://www.chabad.org/library/article_cdo/aid/5068732/jewish/What-Does-the-Last-Name-Levy-Mean.htm")[is the second most common last name in Israel].
@chabad-levy
The "Levin" variation is more common in English-speaking countries like the @US:a

So, in theory: a woman with a seemingly Jewish heritage of the Eastern-European (Ashkenazi) variety most likely born in an
English-speaking country like the @US:a, with a common forename and surname, and somehow connected to Ehud Barak -
a former Israeli general and prime minister.

